===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|turbot}}, {{m|enm|turbut}}, from {{der|en|xno|turbut}}, {{der|en|fro|turbot}}, {{m|fro|torbot}}, from {{der|en|gmq-osw|tornbut}}, from {{m|gmq-osw|törn|t=thorn}} + {{m|gmq-osw|but|t=butt, flatfish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɜːbət/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-turbot.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɝbət/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|turbot|s}}

# A species of [[flatfish]] native to Europe ({{taxlink|Scophthalmus maximus|species}}, earlier {{taxlink|Psetta maxima|species}}).
# Any of various other [[flatfish]]es of family {{ll|mul|Scophthalmidae}} that are found in marine or brackish waters.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931|author=[[w:John Palmer (author)|Francis Beeding]]
|title=Death Walks in Eastrepps
|chapter=1/1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL6342782W
|passage=Eldridge closed the despatch-case with a snap and, rising briskly, walked down the corridor to his solitary table in the dining-car. Mulligatawny soup, poached '''turbot''', roast leg of lamb—the usual railway dinner.}}
# [[triggerfish|Triggerfish]], {{taxlink|Canthidermis sufflamen|species}}.

